  "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with angles that are not all right. So it is **not** a square.\n\n- Since the four sides of the quadrilateral are congruent, it is a rhombus.\n\n- The quadrilateral is a rhombus. A rhombus is a parallelogram where all four sides are equal. So it is a parallelogram.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms II (rhombus), and III (parallelogram).",
